code.code:
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then((res) => res.json())
    .then((user) => setUser(user))
    .catch((err) => {
      console.error('Failed to load user', err);
      setError('Something went wrong');
    });
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [userId]);

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Symptom 1 — a successful request reported as a failure.** `controller.abort()` in the cleanup rejects the pending `fetch` with a `DOMException` named `AbortError`, so every effect re-run (a new `userId`, an unmount, or the mount–cleanup–mount cycle React performs in development) lands in `.catch` and calls `setError`. Cancellation is a decision the app made, not a failure: check `controller.signal.aborted` (or `err.name === 'AbortError'`) first and return before touching error state (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal). Checking the signal also covers a wrapper that rethrows a different error after an abort.

**Symptom 2 — a 404 rendered as a user.** `fetch()` fulfills for 404 and 500; it rejects only for transport failures and aborts. With no `ok` check, `res.json()` runs on the error body: a JSON error payload becomes the “user” (every field `undefined`), and an HTML error page throws inside the chain, which the `catch` reports as the generic message while hiding the real status (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/ok). The status has to be turned into a failure deliberately: `if (!res.ok) throw new HttpError(res.status, res.statusText)`.

**Symptom 3 — the previous user flashing back.** Abort is best effort. Once a response has arrived, the `.then` continuations are already queued and `abort()` cannot unschedule them, so a cleanup that runs after that point leaves a `setUser(oldUser)` in flight. The chain needs its own cancellation flag (or a request id compared just before `setUser`). Both mechanisms stay, because they do different jobs: the signal stops the network work, the flag stops the continuation.

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  const controller = new AbortController();

  (async () => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, { signal: controller.signal });
      if (!res.ok) throw new HttpError(res.status, res.statusText);
      const user = (await res.json()) as User;
      if (cancelled) return;          // a superseded response must not render
      setUser(user);
      setError(null);                 // a recovered request clears the old message
    } catch (err) {
      if (cancelled || controller.signal.aborted) return;  // expected, not a failure
      setError('Something went wrong');
      reportError(err);               // monitoring keeps the real status
    }
  })();

  return () => {
    cancelled = true;
    controller.abort();
  };
}, [userId]);
```

**What the shape guarantees.** No toast for a request the app itself cancelled; HTTP failures surfaced with their status instead of parsed as data; no stale render, because the continuation is gated on the same flag the cleanup sets; and `res.json()` is only reached after `ok`, so an HTML error page or an empty 204 never becomes a “user”. The remaining design question is the error itself: with a single `error` slot, a late failure from an old request would resurface, which is why the flag is checked before `setError` too.

code.code:
self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  event.respondWith(
    caches.open('v1').then(async (cache) => {
      const cached = await cache.match(event.request);
      if (cached) return cached;
      const res = await fetch(event.request);
      cache.put(event.request, res.clone());
      return res;
    })
  );
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Symptom 1 — the shell never updates.** A navigation is a request like any other, so the cached HTML always wins and the network branch only runs on a cache miss — which never happens after the first visit. Nothing in the worker invalidates `'v1'`: a deploy changes files on the server, not entries in the cache. The user is served last month's HTML indefinitely, and new HTML against old hashed assets is the mixed-version failure that follows.

**Symptom 2 — another account's dashboard.** The cache key is the request URL, not the user, and the handler stores *every* response, `/api/me` included. After the kiosk's logout/login, `cache.match('/api/me')` returns the previous account's body. Personalized or authenticated responses must never enter a shared cache: let them through to the network (return without calling `respondWith`, or fetch and skip the `put`), or, when offline access is genuinely required, key the cache per user and delete it on logout.

**Symptom 3 — the browser error page offline.** `respondWith()` receives a promise that rejects when both the cache and the network miss, and a rejected `respondWith` is a network error, so the user sees the browser's page instead of the app. Navigations need a fallback that cannot reject: any navigation, hit or miss, gets the cached shell, with the network preferred when it is available.

**Hazards the reports have not exposed yet.** No `method` guard: `cache.put()` on a POST rejects with a `TypeError` (https://w3c.github.io/ServiceWorker/#cache-put), and that rejection is never awaited or caught, so every write pays a cache lookup and leaves an unhandled rejection. No `res.ok` guard: a 500 HTML page is stored under the URL and then served as if it were the resource. No cleaning: one cache name that never changes, no bound, no `activate` prune, so old entries accumulate forever. And the handler intercepts cross-origin requests (analytics, CDNs) it has no business caching.

```ts
const SHELL = 'shell-v4';
const ASSETS = 'assets-v4';

self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  const { request } = event;
  if (request.method !== 'GET') return;                       // writes: network's business
  const url = new URL(request.url);
  if (url.origin !== self.location.origin) return;            // third parties out of scope
  if (url.pathname.startsWith('/api/')) return;               // authenticated data: network only

  if (request.mode === 'navigate') {
    event.respondWith(networkFirstShell(request));
    return;
  }
  event.respondWith(cacheFirst(request, ASSETS));
});

async function networkFirstShell(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
  const cache = await caches.open(SHELL);
  try {
    const res = await fetch(request);
    if (res.ok) await cache.put(request, res.clone());
    return res;
  } catch {
    return (await cache.match('/')) ?? Response.error();       // offline fallback
  }
}

async function cacheFirst(request: Request, name: string): Promise<Response> {
  const cache = await caches.open(name);
  const hit = await cache.match(request);
  if (hit) return hit;                                         // hashed: name == content
  const res = await fetch(request);
  if (res.ok) await cache.put(request, res.clone());            // never cache errors
  return res;
}

self.addEventListener('activate', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil((async () => {
    for (const key of await caches.keys()) {
      if (key !== SHELL && key !== ASSETS) await caches.delete(key);
    }
    await self.clients.claim();
  })());
});
```

**Why the routing is the fix, not a style choice.** Each class has a different cost of being wrong. HTML is the entry point: serving it stale breaks the deploy, so it is network-first with a shell fallback. Hashed assets cannot go stale — the filename changes with the bytes — so cache-first is free. Slowly-changing data tolerates a stale-while-revalidate window, but only if the cached copy was an `ok` response. Authenticated data cannot be cached at all without per-user keying and a purge on logout. And the versioned names plus the `activate` sweep are what make a deploy actually replace the previous release.

code.code:
function openDB() {
  const req = indexedDB.open('app', 2);
  req.onupgradeneeded = () => {
    const db = req.result;
    if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains('notes')) {
      db.createObjectStore('notes', { keyPath: 'id' });
    }
    const store = db.transaction.objectStore('notes');
    if (!store.indexNames.contains('byUpdated')) {
      store.createIndex('byUpdated', 'updatedAt');
    }
  };
  req.onsuccess = () => { /* the db exists */ };
  return req;
}

// call sites
const db = openDB();
// await openDB() -> an IDBOpenDBRequest, not an IDBDatabase

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Defect 1 — `db.transaction` is the method, not the transaction (symptom 1).** During the upgrade the running version-change transaction is `request.transaction`; `db.transaction` is the `transaction()` *function*, so `.objectStore` is `undefined` and the call throws. The throw aborts the version-change transaction, the open request then fires `error` (an `AbortError`), `onsuccess` never runs, and the app waits forever. Fix: `const store = req.transaction!.objectStore('notes');` — or keep the `IDBObjectStore` that `createObjectStore()` returns instead of looking it up again.

**Defect 2 — the wrapper hands back an `IDBOpenDBRequest` (symptom 3).** Opening is asynchronous, and the request is not a database: it has no `transaction()`, and `result` is only populated in `onsuccess`. A wrapper owes its callers a `Promise<IDBDatabase>` that resolves in `onsuccess` and rejects in `onerror`.

**Defect 3 — no `onblocked` (part of symptom 2).** The version-change transaction is exclusive: while another connection holds the database the upgrade waits, and the upgrading tab's request never reaches `success`. `onblocked` is the only signal that this is happening, and it must become a user-visible state (“close the other tabs”) plus a retry, not silence.

**Defect 4 — no `versionchange` handler on open connections (the rest of symptom 2).** The other side of the same deadlock: a tab that opened v1 keeps its connection open and blocks the upgrade forever. Every open connection must react with `db.onversionchange = () => { db.close(); notifyReloadNeeded(); }`, so the waiting upgrade can proceed and that tab can reopen at the new version. `onblocked` alone informs; `versionchange` is what unblocks.

**Defect 5 — the smaller omissions.** `req.onerror` is unhandled, so quota failures and aborted upgrades disappear. `event.oldVersion` is ignored, so the code cannot tell a fresh install from an upgrade and has no place to migrate records. And `req.onsuccess` is an empty comment, so nothing hands the database over.

```ts
export const DB_VERSION = 2;

export function openDb(): Promise<IDBDatabase> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const request = indexedDB.open('app', DB_VERSION);

    request.onupgradeneeded = (event) => {
      const db = request.result;
      let notes: IDBObjectStore;

      if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains('notes')) {
        notes = db.createObjectStore('notes', { keyPath: 'id' });
      } else {
        notes = request.transaction!.objectStore('notes');   // the versionchange transaction
      }

      if (!notes.indexNames.contains('byUpdated')) {
        notes.createIndex('byUpdated', 'updatedAt');
      }

      if (event.oldVersion < 2) {
        // v1 -> v2 record migration goes here, in the same transaction
      }
    };

    request.onblocked = () => notifyCloseOtherTabs();    // upgrade is waiting on a connection

    request.onsuccess = () => {
      const db = request.result;
      db.onversionchange = () => {                       // let a newer version through
        db.close();
        notifyReloadNeeded();
      };
      resolve(db);
    };

    request.onerror = () => reject(request.error);
  });
}
```

**Why this closes the loop.** The upgrade body now runs in the transaction that exists, so `onsuccess` is reachable; the promise boundary means callers cannot receive a request; and the two-tab case becomes a conversation — the upgrading tab reports `blocked`, the older tab closes on `versionchange` and reloads. Keeping the `contains()` guards matters because the same code runs for a fresh database and for an upgrade, and because the version-change transaction is atomic: any throw rolls the schema back to the previous version instead of leaving a half-migrated store.

code.code:
// tab, on save
localStorage.setItem('note:' + id, JSON.stringify(note));

// tab, on clear all
function clearAll() {
  localStorage.clear();
}

// every tab
window.addEventListener('storage', (e) => {
  if (e.key?.startsWith('note:')) refreshFromServer();
});

// assumption: the service worker listens for 'storage' too, so its offline cache
// stays in sync with the notes

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Report 1 — the service worker never hears any of this.** The `storage` event is fired on `Window` objects of the same origin and nowhere else; a service worker has no `window`, never receives it, and cannot read `localStorage` at all. The comment describes something that cannot happen, so the worker's cache keeps the deleted note and serves it offline. Any cross-context signal has to use a channel both sides can join: `BroadcastChannel` works in windows, dedicated and shared workers, and service workers.

**Report 2 — `clear()` fires one event with `key === null`.** `localStorage.clear()` removes every key and delivers a single `storage` event whose `key` is `null`, so `e.key?.startsWith('note:')` is `undefined` and the handler returns without refreshing (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/storage_event). The second half of the same report is the event's audience: it fires in the *other* documents of the origin, never in the one that wrote, so the tab that cleared never refreshes through this path either. Handling deletion means treating `key === null` as “everything changed” (and `newValue === null` as a single removal), and updating local state on the write path instead of waiting for an echo.

**Report 3 — a notification is not conflict resolution.** Two tabs read the same note, each writes its own version, and the last write wins: no channel serialises the writes, and `localStorage` has no compare-and-set. A broadcast only tells peers that something changed; it cannot prevent the loss. The fix belongs where the truth lives — a version or `ETag` the server checks, a queue that serialises writes, or an explicit last-write-wins rule with UI that shows the conflict.

**What the channel does and does not guarantee.** Delivery is fire-and-forget and only to live contexts: a closed tab misses the message and nothing replays it, so every receiver re-reads durable state instead of trusting a payload, and every handler is idempotent. Messages carry signals (`{ type: 'note-changed', id, version }`), not the note itself.

```ts
// durable state in IndexedDB: readable from tabs and from the service worker
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('notes-sync-v1');

export async function saveNote(note: Note) {
  const saved = await db.put('notes', { ...note, updatedAt: Date.now() });
  channel.postMessage({ v: 1, type: 'note-changed', id: saved.id });
  renderNote(saved);                       // the writer never gets its own event
}

export async function clearAll() {
  await db.clear('notes');
  channel.postMessage({ v: 1, type: 'all-cleared' });
  renderEmptyState();
}

channel.addEventListener('message', async ({ data }) => {
  if (data.type === 'note-changed') renderNote(await db.get('notes', data.id));
  if (data.type === 'all-cleared') renderAll(await db.getAll('notes'));
});

// service worker, sw.ts: same channel, so a delete can also purge the served copy.
// Top level, not inside install: worker globals are recreated on every start.
const notesChannel = new BroadcastChannel('notes-sync-v1');

notesChannel.addEventListener('message', async ({ data }) => {
  if (data.type !== 'note-changed' && data.type !== 'all-cleared') return;
  const cache = await caches.open('notes-v1');
  if (data.id) await cache.delete(`/notes/${data.id}`);
});
```

**Why this arrangement holds.** One durable store readable from every context (IndexedDB) removes the copy that could disagree; `BroadcastChannel` reaches the worker, which `storage` cannot; signals instead of payloads make a lost message a delayed refresh rather than stale data; and conflict resolution stays on the server, where a version can actually be compared. The `storage` event remains a fine shortcut for tab-to-tab invalidation if the app keeps a `localStorage` mirror — but it must handle `key === null`, and it can never be the worker's channel.

code.code:
async function consumeEvents(url: string, onEvent: (e: unknown) => void) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  const reader = response.body!.getReader();
  const decoder = new TextDecoder();

  for (;;) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;

    const text = decoder.decode(value);
    for (const line of text.split("\n")) {
      if (line) onEvent(JSON.parse(line));
    }
  }
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Three independent defects, all at the same layer — the assumption that a chunk is a record.

1. **HTTP status is never checked.** `fetch()` fulfills for 404 and 500; only transport failures reject. `response.body!` also lies: a `204` or `HEAD`-like response has no body and `getReader()` would throw on `null`. Check `if (!response.ok || !response.body) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`)` before touching the stream, so an HTML error page is never fed to `JSON.parse`.

2. **Chunks are transport boundaries, not line boundaries.** A record can be split across two `read()` calls; splitting per chunk then parsing the truncated tail throws `Unexpected end of JSON input`, and the head of the next record is silently dropped. Keep a `pending` string across iterations: append decoded text, split on `\n`, parse everything except the last element, and carry that last element forward. Flush `pending` after the loop ends.

3. **One decoder, stream mode.** `decode(value)` treats each chunk as a complete UTF-8 byte sequence. An emoji is four bytes and `é` is two; when the boundary falls inside a code point, the partial bytes decode to U+FFFD — the mojibake. Use a single `TextDecoder` created outside the loop and call `decoder.decode(value, { stream: !done })`, which keeps the incomplete bytes buffered; the final call with `done === true` (stream false) flushes them. That final flush is also where the last `pending` line gets completed.

4. **Abandoning the stream.** A `throw` from `JSON.parse` or from `onEvent` escapes the loop with the reader still locked and the connection open. Wrap the loop in `try/finally` and `await reader.cancel()` on the way out — or better, pass an `AbortSignal` and abort the whole request — and decide a per-record policy for malformed lines instead of letting one bad record kill the stream. Backpressure needs no extra code: awaiting each `read()` and processing the chunk before requesting the next one is what keeps memory bounded.

Corrected shape:

```ts
const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
if (!res.ok || !res.body) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let pending = "";
try {
  for (;;) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    pending += decoder.decode(value, { stream: !done });
    const lines = pending.split("\n");
    pending = lines.pop() ?? "";
    for (const line of lines) if (line) consume(line);
    if (done) break;
  }
  if (pending) consume(pending);
} finally {
  await reader.cancel().catch(() => {});
}
```

Sources: MDN `Response.body` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/body) and the Streams API (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Streams_API).

code.code:
window.addEventListener("beforeunload", () => {
  navigator.sendBeacon("/analytics", JSON.stringify({ type: "hide" }));
  saveDraft(); // async POST /drafts/:id
});

document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => {
  if (document.visibilityState === "visible") resumeTransientWork();
});

window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
  stopPolling();
  cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The code uses `beforeunload` as if it were a reliable save hook, and treats `pagehide` as a teardown when it may mean "the page is going into the back/forward cache and will be resumed".

1. **Symptom 1 — the save never runs.** `beforeunload` is not guaranteed: mobile browsers skip it or fire it when the app is already being frozen, and an in-flight `fetch()` in a dying document is killed. The reliable moment is `visibilitychange` to `hidden`, which fires when the user switches apps, tabs, or backgrounds the browser; `pagehide` is the compatible fallback for real navigations. Save there, and make the save durable and idempotent — a local write (IndexedDB/localStorage) plus a queued sync that a service worker can flush later, keyed by draft id so a double save is harmless. `sendBeacon()` is right for the small analytics payload and belongs in that hidden/pagehide path; it is not a substitute for persisting application state.

2. **Symptom 2 — no prompt because there is no warning.** `beforeunload` shows a confirmation only if the handler calls `preventDefault()` (legacy: sets `returnValue`). This handler does neither; it just saves. The correct pattern is conditional: register the listener only while unsaved data exists, call `preventDefault()` in it, and remove the listener as soon as the draft is saved — an always-on `beforeunload` listener both nags and can cost back/forward-cache eligibility.

3. **Symptom 3 — teardown on a page that is being cached.** When the page enters the back/forward cache, `pagehide` fires with `event.persisted === true`, and the same document is later restored with a `pageshow` event (`persisted === true`, `event.persisted` on pageshow too). Unconditionally calling `stopPolling()` and `cancelAnimationFrame()` there kills the work, and since nothing resumes it, the restored page is dead until a reload. Pause on `pagehide` (regardless of `persisted`) and resume on `pageshow`; when `persisted` is true, skip re-boot logic that would duplicate listeners. The same guard applies to `resumeTransientWork()` in the `visibilitychange` handler — it resumes but nothing pauses.

Rewritten shape:

```js
let dirty = false;

const warn = (event) => { event.preventDefault(); event.returnValue = true; };
function setDirty(next) {
  dirty = next;
  window[next ? "addEventListener" : "removeEventListener"]("beforeunload", warn);
}

function persist() {
  if (!dirty) return;
  saveDraftLocally();                  // durable, idempotent, keyed by id
  navigator.sendBeacon("/analytics", JSON.stringify({ type: "hide" }));
  setDirty(false);
}

document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => {
  if (document.visibilityState === "hidden") { persist(); pauseTransientWork(); }
  else resumeTransientWork();
});

window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
  persist();
  pauseTransientWork();               // teardown the loop, keep the state
});

window.addEventListener("pageshow", (event) => {
  if (event.persisted) resumeTransientWork();  // restored, not re-booted
});
```

Sources: MDN `visibilitychange` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/visibilitychange_event), `pagehide` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/pagehide_event), `beforeunload` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/beforeunload_event).
